{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>All things.</b> Some interpret יגעים as though [it meant] full, but this is impossible, since יגעים is an active participle, like \"the one who is weary and exhausted.\" If the meaning were as they interpret it, it would be מיגעים in the pi'el with a dagesh, from \"do not toil there\" (לא תיגע שמה). Rather, יגעים returns upon the things themselves and the object — that they are weary and lacking; there is no power in them; therefore no man can speak. And after he has made known the four roots, that they stand upon their pattern — and if change (תנועה) occurs to them at the end, they return as at first — truth we have known: the universals and the particulars generated from them do not stand for a single moment upon one pattern; therefore a man cannot count them. And the eye is not sated from seeing them, for the cause of the eye's seeing is forms, which are images reflected in the clear air, and they do not stand for a moment. And the ear is not filled from hearing the particulars, for also the cause of hearing is the drawing in of air in which there are forms of sounds — these too do not stand; therefore the eye cannot grasp their detail, nor the ear hear their number, for there is no end or number to them among the children of men. Therefore the Creator alone knows the universal and the particular, for all are the work of His hands."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:26:52.350226630Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}